In C, **a file** refers to an external data storage object—such as a text document, binary image, log file, configuration file, etc.—that resides outside your program’s memory space and persists between runs.  
C provides a standard library interface (`stdio.h`) for working with files through *streams* represented by objects of type `FILE`.  

### Key Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **File Stream (`FILE` pointer)** | An opaque structure used internally by the run‑time library to keep track of open files. You never create it directly; you obtain one via `fopen()`/`freopen()` and release it with `fclose()`. |
| **Opening a File** | ```c
   FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt","r"); /* read mode */
   ```
   Modes include `"r"` (read), `"w"` (write – truncates if exists), `"a"` (append), plus variants like `"rb"` / `"wb"` for binary access. |
| **Reading/Writing Text vs Binary** | Use `fprintf`, `fscanf`, `puts`, `gets` (unsafe), `fgetc`, `fputc`, `getline`, etc., for textual data. For raw bytes use `fread`, `fwrite`. |
| **Closing a File** | Always call `fclose(fp);` when finished to flush buffers and free resources. |
| **Error Handling** | Functions return `NULL` or negative values on failure; check `errno` or use `perror()` for diagnostics. |
| **Positioning & Size** | `ftell(fp)` gives current offset; `rewind(fp)` resets to start; `fseek(fp,offset,SEEK_SET/SEEK_CUR/SEEK_END)` moves cursor. |
| **Buffered vs Unbuffered** | By default streams are buffered. Call `setbuf(fp,NULL)` or `setvbuf(...)` to change buffering behavior. |

### Typical Workflow Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    const char *filename = "data.bin";
    FILE *in  = fopen(filename,"rb");
    FILE *out = fopen("copy.dat","wb");

    if (!in || !out) {
        perror("Failed to open file(s)");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    unsigned char buffer[4096];
    size_t n;
    while ((n = fread(buffer,1,sizeof buffer,in)) > 0) {
        fwrite(buffer,1,n,out);
    }

    fclose(in);
    fclose(out);

    printf("Copy completed.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

This snippet opens two binary files, copies contents from source to destination, handles errors, and closes both streams properly.

---

#### Quick Reference Table

| Function | Purpose | Return Value |
|----------|--------|--------------|
| `fopen(const char *,const char *)` | Open file | `FILE *` (or `NULL`) |
| `fclose(FILE *)` | Close stream | `EOF` on error, otherwise `0` |
| `fread(void*,size_t,size_t,FILE*)` | Read into buffer | Number of elements successfully read |
| `fwrite(const void*,size_t,size_t,FILE*)` | Write from buffer | Number written |
| `fprintf(FILE*,const char*,...)` | Formatted output | Negative on error |
| `fscanf(FILE*,const char*,... )` | Scanned input | Number matched |
| `feof(FILE *)` | End-of-file indicator | Non-zero if EOF reached |
| `ferror(FILE *)` | Error flag | Non-zero if error occurred |
| `fflush(FILE *)` | Flush write buffer | `EOF` on error |

Understanding how to manipulate files this way lets you perform tasks ranging from simple logging to complex binary serialization—all within the portable confines of ANSI/C99+ standards.
